# Language and UI Text

## Comments and code docs — English only

- All `//`, `///`, `/* */`, and `#region` labels in `.cs` files: **English**
- XAML `<!-- comments -->`: **English**
- Do not add Vietnamese in source comments, even for vision/robot logic

```csharp
// ✅ Segment PCB by contour area threshold
// ❌ Phân đoạn PCB theo ngưỡng diện tích contour
```

Identifiers (types, methods, properties) stay **English** per existing conventions.

## Operator-facing text — Vietnamese

User-visible strings (XAML `Text`/`Content`/`Header`, `MessageBox`, status bar, tooltips) stay **Vietnamese** per `memory-bank/productContext.md`.

- Use natural, concise operator language — not literal machine translation
- Keep domain terms consistent: **giống** / **khác**, **Tạo mẫu**, **Xem mẫu**, **mẫu**, **vùng**, **độ tương đồng**
- Prefer sentence case for labels; avoid mixing English UI words unless they are established product terms (e.g. ROI, FPS)

## Font for Vietnamese

Vietnamese UI must render full diacritics (ă, â, ê, ô, ơ, ư, đ).

| Use | Font |
|-----|------|
| Labels, buttons, dialogs, status | `{DynamicResource MaterialDesignFont}` — inherit from `MainWindow` / tab root |
| Numeric / joint / debug readouts only | `Consolas` or other monospace |

- Do **not** set `FontFamily="Consolas"` (or other Latin-only fonts) on Vietnamese `TextBlock` / `Button` / `Label`
- New windows and tabs: set `FontFamily="{DynamicResource MaterialDesignFont}"` on the root element (see `MainWindow.xaml`)
- If a control overrides font, use `MaterialDesignFont` or `Segoe UI` — both support Vietnamese on Windows

## When editing UI copy

1. Read surrounding strings in the same view for tone and terminology
2. Verify diacritics are present and spelled correctly (common fixes: *mẫu* not *mau*, *độ* not *do*)
3. Run or visually check the screen if wording changed materially
